WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
In information theory, there's a standard measurement

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.045
called "mutual information,"

00:00:05.045 --> 00:00:08.920
for how many bits of info travel from source to destination.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:11.260
If a monkey hears another monkey's call,

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.420
let's say that's 1 bit.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.820
So a monkey sees an eagle and calls to its friend.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.580
The friend hears the call. Presumably, it hides.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.460
But what if the second monkey hears the "snake" alert,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:23.920
instead of eagle?

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.220
The message is still 1 bit, but its meaning has been transformed.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.355
Mutual information measures don't distinguish between

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.840
these two messages, even though they have very different meanings.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.680
In their new paper, information theorists Artemy Kolchinsky &amp; Bernat Corominas-Murtra

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.060
present a new way to think about this that distinguishes between copied information

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.320
and transformed information.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.520
This distinction between copied and transformed information

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.140
arises in our computers,

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.700
when we either copy files or encrypt them.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.760
It also arises in biology, where DNA is both replicated

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.500
and transcribed.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.120
And of course, in animal communication.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.080
According to the authors, modeling these different types of information

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.940
might help us better understand these processes,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.440
to the benefit of monkeys everywhere.

